SPDX-FileNotice: retrods local patch, not part of upstream gambatte-libretro.

Guard the global NetSerial instance with HAVE_NETWORK.

The body of the serial-link setup is already inside `#ifdef HAVE_NETWORK`, but
the file-scope `static NetSerial gb_net_serial;` is not. Even with netplay
disabled that definition still emits a constructor/destructor reference, which
fails to link once net_serial.cpp is dropped (netplay needs BSD sockets, which
the DS does not have).

Applied with: patch -p1 < patches/gambatte-libretro/0001-netplay-optional.patch

0002-ds-native-pixels.patch

Add VIDEO_ABGR1555_OPAQUE. The core already has a VIDEO_ABGR1555 output mode
(written for the PS2), which is the DS's own pixel layout except that a DS
bitmap background treats bit 15 as "opaque". With this define every palette
colour carries that bit, so retrods can DMA the frame straight into VRAM with
no per-pixel conversion.

0003-decimated-psg.patch

Add GAMBATTE_DECIMATED_PSG. The sound channels write their level changes into
a buffer at the native rate (~2 MHz, 35112 slots a frame), which the core then
integrates and the frontend resamples: three passes over every slot, which on
the DS's slow main RAM cost about as much as emulating the rest of the machine.
With this define the channels write through a small cursor type (src/sound/
psg_buf.h) that drops each change straight into the ~32 kHz output slot it
falls in, weighted for a box average of those 64 native samples, and the
blipper is bypassed. A leaky integrator matching the blipper's removes the
channels' DC offset. On the host the output correlates with the original at
0.999 (about 1.33x louder); on a DSi it took Tetris from 45 ms to 11-13 ms a
frame.
